test.yaml:2:3: trigger "pull_request_target" is dangerous: it runs in the context of the base repository with the default token of the repository (it can write unless the settings of the repository restrict it) and no secrets, even when the pull request comes from a fork. use "pull_request" unless write access is required, and never check out or run code of the pull request [dangerous-triggers]
test.yaml:4:3: trigger "workflow_run" is dangerous: it runs in the context of the default branch with the default token of the repository (it can write unless the settings of the repository restrict it) and no secrets, but processes artifacts and metadata produced by a run that a fork may control. consider a reusable workflow called with "workflow_call" instead, and never trust the artifacts or the event payload [dangerous-triggers]
test.yaml:7:3: trigger "issue_comment" is dangerous: it runs in the context of the default branch with the default token of the repository (it can write unless the settings of the repository restrict it) and no secrets, and it can be started by anyone who can comment. check the permission of the commenter and never run code of the pull request [dangerous-triggers]
